{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.BarParityExclusion","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BarParityExclusion","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.barEven_cannot_carry_barOdd_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.barEven_cannot_carry_barOdd_split","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BarParityExclusion.lean#L46-L56","doc":"**I-2 exclusion.** If the proton−neutron trace difference `d` is bar-ODD and\nnonzero at some point, then no bar-EVEN candidate `c` equals it: a bar-even\ncorrection cannot carry the p≠n binding split. (Compute: the intra-nucleon p/n\ndistinction is `+h` per nucleon — bar-odd — per `he3_tritium_cosh_asymmetry_diagnosis`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.barEven_and_barOdd_forces_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.barEven_and_barOdd_forces_zero","kind":"theorem","line":35,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BarParityExclusion.lean#L35-L44","doc":"A function that is simultaneously bar-EVEN (`f(−h) = f h`) and bar-ODD\n(`f(−h) = −f h`) is identically zero. The two parity conditions force\n`f h = −f h`, hence `2·f h = 0`, hence `f h = 0` since `2 ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}